  "title": "✈️ Fly to Brandenburg",
  "date": "2025-11-08T22:00:00.000Z",
  "content": "✈️ Fly to Brandenburg\n\nIt’s finally time for my Erasmus trip to Brandenburg! A small journey through four cities — Tallinn, Riga, Berlin, and finally Brandenburg.\n\n🇪🇪 Tallinn Airport\n\nWaiting at Tallinn Airport is surprisingly fun! 🎹 There’s a piano you can play, two table tennis tables, and even a free gym inside the airport.\n\nAnd a fun fact — smoking rooms do exist in some European airports! Meaning yes, you can actually bring your lighter on board. 🔥\n\npiano\ngym\n\nWhen the plane took off, I finally saw the sunlight above the clouds — something rare after weeks of grey skies in Tallinn. ☁️✨\nThe clouds looked like giant marshmallows. I always wonder how pilots see anything through them...\n\nclouds\n\n🇱🇻 Riga Airport\n\nWhat shocked me most in Riga Airport? A giant bear display showing what items are illegal to bring into Europe. 🧸🚫\n\nbear\n\nYou can buy mini 50ml whisky bottles and cigarettes right at the duty-free shop — classic European travel essentials. 🥃🚬\n\nwhisky\ncigar\n\nI had a 3-hour layover here. Because of new entry rules for non-EU residents, I decided to stay in the airport. Luckily, Riga Airport has comfortable seats with charging ports (Type-C and Type-A) and a great view. 🔌✨\n\nresting seats\n\nI also spotted some stylish airplanes on the runway.\n\nnice plane\n\nEven more exciting — AirBaltic offers free Starlink Wi-Fi! 🚀 The connection is fast enough for video calls, which feels futuristic at 30,000 feet.\n\nstarlink\n\nDuring the safety video, I even saw VR headsets mentioned — not allowed during takeoff, but still cool to see aviation adapting to new tech. 🥽✈️\n\nheadsets\n\n🇩🇪 Berlin Airport\n\nMy first impression of Berlin Airport? Honestly… big but a bit dirty 😅.\n\nI couldn’t even find a “Welcome to Berlin” sign — kind of disappointing for such a famous city.\n\nThe train\n\nThe public transport system here is confusing — multiple companies, multiple apps, and two types of ticketing systems. 🤯 Even worse, some apps don’t fully support English, making it frustrating for tourists.\n\ninformation board\n\nAnd yes, the ticket machines are literally running on unactivated Windows systems. 💀💻\n\nticket service\n\n🏡 Arrived at Brandenburg\n\nAfter almost 2 hours on the train, I finally arrived in Brandenburg — tired and starving. It was 11 p.m. on a Sunday, so most restaurants were closed. Luckily, I found a place called Palace Pizza — a kebab pizza for €10 saved my night. 🍕❤️\n\nkebab pizza\n\nBrandenburg isn’t actually that small — its market size feels bigger than Tallinn, and surprisingly, everyday items in the cheapest level are cheaper too.\n\nAlso, the floors are starting from zero.\n\n💶 I’ll share more about the city in my next post!",
